# api/.dockerignore — defence in depth, NOT the primary control.
#
# ── WHAT THE PRIMARY CONTROL ACTUALLY IS ─────────────────────────────────────
# The build CONTEXT. `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds with `context: ./api`,
# and a Docker build cannot COPY a byte that the context does not contain. So
# `models/` (304 MB of .gguf), `.git/`, `.terraform/`, `.env`, `crash.log` and
# everything else at the REPOSITORY ROOT are already unreachable from this
# Dockerfile by construction. That is the real boundary, and it is a property of
# the context path, not of any list in this file.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ANYWAY ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Because the context is a property of the BUILD INVOCATION, not of this
# directory. The context is written in one place — release.yml's build job — and
# the Dockerfile cannot enforce it. Change that one line to `context: .` and
# every forbidden artifact in the repository becomes COPY-able in a single edit,
# with no other file objecting.
#
# The concrete hole this closes TODAY, with no context change at all: the
# Dockerfile does `COPY app/ ./app/`, so ANY `.env`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.gguf`
# or `.tfstate` dropped into `api/app/` — by a developer debugging locally, by a
# stray editor backup, by a test fixture someone forgot to delete — is swept into
# the image by that one line. This file makes that impossible regardless of what
# the context is.
#
# It is a second line of defence behind the context, never in front of it. If
# the context ever stops being `./api`, this file is what stands between the
# repository's secrets and a published image — and the image-content gate
# (`.github/workflows/image-content-gate.py`) is the third layer, which checks
# the BUILT image rather than the context.
#
# ── WHY `**/` IS REPEATED ON EVERY PATTERN ───────────────────────────────────
# Docker matches these patterns with `filepath.Match` semantics, where `*` does
# NOT cross a path separator: `*.gguf` matches `/models/x.gguf` no more than it
# matches `/app/deep/x.gguf`. Every pattern that must hold at any depth is
# therefore written twice, once bare and once with the `**/` prefix. This is the
# same basename-vs-full-path trap Stage 1's hygiene gate documents, and getting
# it wrong is silent.
#
# ── `.env` IS ANCHORED EXACTLY, LIKE THE HYGIENE GATE'S ─────────────────────
# `.env.example` is a committed, credential-free template and must keep being
# COPY-able if a build ever wants it. Matching `.env*` would be the wrong rule.

# ── Rule 1: forbidden names, mirroring the image-content gate exactly ────────
.git
**/.git
*.gguf
**/*.gguf
*.tfstate
**/*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
**/*.tfstate.*
.env
**/.env
*.pem
**/*.pem
*.key
**/*.key
crash.log
**/crash.log

# Terraform provider cache and state, which live at the repo root and could be
# reached by any future edit that widens the context.
.terraform
**/.terraform

# ── Build and interpreter residue ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not a secret class. These are artefacts of running tests on a workstation, and
# they are currently being baked into the published image: `api/app/__pycache__`
# exists on disk right now with CPython 3.14 bytecode in it, which this
# python:3.12-slim image can never use and which would ship a second, stale
# copy of every module alongside the real one.
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
**/*.pyc
*.pyo
**/*.pyo
.pytest_cache
**/.pytest_cache

# Editor and OS droppings.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
*.swp
**/*.swp
*~

# ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED ────────────────────────────────────────
# `app/` itself, `requirements.txt`, `Dockerfile`, `alembic/`, `alembic.ini` and
# `tests/` are all still COPY-able. Nothing in this file may make the image
# unbuildable, and an exclusion that broke `COPY requirements.txt ./` would trade
# a leak for an outage.